          from the collection on 2026-09-12: it could not read a             CRUNCHED member, which is what nearly every real archive             holds.  Four genuine 1980s archives and one written by this             disk's own `arc' were tried and every one stopped at the             first member with `File not packed with correct number of             bits'.  `arc' reads them all.  compress   you want the classic `.Z'.  `compr' is a second, smaller             implementation of the same thing.             CMDS/compress's output through a pipe comes out corrupt, in             both directions; write it to a file, or take compr or             REBUILT/compress_rebuilt, which are right either way.  gzip       you want `.gz'.  Six builds of it are on this disk under             CMDS/REBUILT for different CPUs and runtimes -- gzip68k,             gzip020, gzipcpu32, each with and without csl.  Take the plain             `gzip' unless you know which of those you need.  ar         you want a Unix-style object library.  OS-9's own libraries             are different things; see `liborder' and `unpacklib'.IF YOU CAN TAKE ONLY ONE FILE`tar', 22,966 bytes.  It needs nothing else on the disk.ONE HAZARD IF YOU TAKE SEVERAL------------------------------OS-9 answers by MODULE NAME once a module is resident, and two files withthe same module name are two paths to one program.  In this family everymodule name is distinct EXCEPT that `lha' reports itself as `LHa' -- whichis the author's own capitalisation and is not a collision, but does mean`mdir' will not show you an `lha'.  `tools/module_census.py' lists everysuch name on the disk.WHAT EACH WANTS BESIDES ITSELFNothing in this family reads a configuration file or a termcap.  `tar' and`gtar' write into the current data directory, so `chd' where you want thefiles before unpacking -- and on this disk bash's `cd' will not do that foryou interactively; use `ksh -c "cd <dir>; tar ..."'.  DOC/README-SHELLS.